#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_shortest_tour

#% text
# `find_shortest_tour(xs)` gives `[length, tour]`: the shortest closed tour through the elements of `xs`, as their positions from 1 back to 1.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `find_shortest_tour(xs, j, k)` gives the shortest path from `xs[j]` to `xs[k]` through all the other elements.

#% item
# Points (lists of numbers) are at their Euclidean distance, numbers at `abs(x - y)`, strings at their edit distance; `distance_function: f` gives the distance `f(a, b)` (which may be `inf` for no step).

#% item
# The tour is exact: dynamic programming over the subsets of the points (Bellman 1962; Held and Karp 1962), for up to 20 elements; a longer list stays unevaluated. The length is the exact sum of the distances along the tour.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
find_shortest_tour([[0, 0], [2, 0], [1, 1], [2, 2], [0, 2]])
#% output
#= [6 + 2*2^(1/2), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 1]]

#% cell
find_shortest_tour([1, 5, 2, 9, 3])
#% output
#= [16, [1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 1]]

#% cell
find_shortest_tour(["cat", "dog", "do", "cap", "dock"])
#% output
#= [11, [1, 2, 3, 5, 4, 1]]

#% cell
find_shortest_tour([[0, 0], [1, 0], [1, 1], [0, 1]], 1, 4)
#% output
#= [3, [1, 2, 3, 4]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
